# Adjudicated `punused` findings for deadset-go. One reason per group; entries
# match as literal substrings of the finding line, so they never include
# file:line:col -- line numbers shift and an entry with one silently goes stale.
#
# `punused` resolves references through gopls inside this module only and does
# not model interface satisfaction, so two whole classes below are its known
# blind spots rather than dead code. Cross-checked at adjudication time:
# `deadcode -test ./...` reports nothing in this module.
#
# Invoke it as `punused` or `punused '**/*.go'`. A package pattern reports
# nothing and exits 0, which reads exactly like a clean scan.

# --- Fixture modules under internal/load/testdata/.
#
# Each of these is a declaration inside a fixture module the loader's own tests
# load and analyze. They are deliberately unreferenced: the fixtures exist to be
# read by a package loader, not called, and two of them are deliberately
# ill-formed (a type mismatch and an undefined identifier) so the fail-closed
# error walk has something to walk. The three in the consumed target are the
# declarations a loaded consumer references, so the module that declares them
# names none of them itself, which is the shape the consumer load is about. Go's
# own tooling ignores a `testdata` directory; this scan does not, so the fixtures
# surface here as findings. Deleting any of them would break the test that names
# it.
function Selected
function Answer
function Mismatch
function Undefined
function Used is unused
function UsedByConsumerTest
function Unused is unused

# --- Methods a standard interface calls, never a caller by name.
#
# `Error()` on both error types satisfies `error`, and production code returns
# them as `error`; `String()` on the kind enumerations satisfies `fmt.Stringer`
# and is what renders a kind in a message and in a report; `Unwrap()` is what
# `errors.Is` and `errors.As` call to walk a chain; `MarshalJSON` and
# `UnmarshalJSON` on the report envelope satisfy `json.Marshaler` and
# `json.Unmarshaler`, which is what makes marshalling an envelope write the
# Contract's document and reading one refuse a member the schema does not
# declare; `Set` satisfies `flag.Value`, and `flag.FlagSet.Var` is what calls it
# once per occurrence of the repeatable format flag. `String()` on the cascade
# spelling is the same shape as the kind enumerations and is reached the same way:
# a verb prints the depth a component listing was made at through a `%s`
# conversion, so the call is the formatting package's and not a call by name.
# `Import` satisfies `go/types.Importer` and is what answers an import path a file
# importing "C" writes, so the second check of such a package is what calls it,
# once per path, through the field it is assigned to.
# No call site exists for a reference search to find in any of these cases,
# because the runtime dispatches through the interface. Removing one would not
# fail the build, it would silently change what a diagnostic prints, which errors
# match, which bytes a report is written as, or which formats an invocation asks
# for, which is why each is pinned by a test of its rendered output, of the match,
# of the round trip, or of the parse.
method (*Error).Error
method (ErrorKind).String
method (RefKind).String
method (Cascade).String
method (*MalformedError).Unwrap
method (*Envelope).MarshalJSON
method (*Envelope).UnmarshalJSON
method (*formatList).Set
method (*cgoImporter).Import

# --- The per-analyzer configuration section.
#
# `Go` is the section of the configuration document this analyzer owns, declared
# in the published schema and therefore accepted by the decode. Nothing reads it
# yet because no setting inside it has an implementation; the field must exist
# for a document that carries the section to resolve rather than be refused.
field Go is unused

# --- What the sweep records per symbol and no finding names one by one.
#
# The graph records which loaded consumers reference one symbol against the day a
# finding names them; a finding carries how many were loaded, so no verb asks the
# question per symbol and only the tests call it. The correct resolution is that
# finding rather than an unexport, because the record is a stage of the same
# package the enumeration and the roots are stages of.
method (*Graph).ConsumersOf
